          "text": "# Plan: AI-Generated Basic Go HTTP Server (Networks Class Project)\n\n## 1. Development Plan (Stages)\n\n**Stage 0 — Project Scaffold**\n- `go mod init <module>` (e.g., `httpserver-lab`)\n- Standard layout: `main.go`, `handlers/`, `middleware/`, `internal/`\n- Pin Go version in `go.mod` (e.g., `go 1.22`) so enhanced `ServeMux` routing is available\n\n**Stage 1 — Minimal Working Server**\n- `http.Server` with `Addr`, `Handler`, `ReadTimeout`, `WriteTimeout`, `IdleTimeout`\n- `http.NewServeMux()` with a few routes: `/`, `/health`, `/echo`\n- Return proper status codes: `200 OK`, `404 Not Found`, `405 Method Not Allowed`, `400 Bad Request`, `500 Internal Server Error`\n\n**Stage 2 — Request/Response Handling**\n- Parse query params, JSON body, path params (Go 1.22 `{id}` syntax)\n- Content-Type negotiation, `http.Error`, JSON encoder with status\n- Consistent error response shape `{ \"error\": \"...\", \"code\": 400 }`\n\n**Stage 3 — Middleware**\n- Logging (method, path, status, duration) — ties to in-class HTTP/transport concepts\n- Recovery (panic → 500)\n- Request ID, CORS (if needed)\n\n**Stage 4 — Graceful Shutdown**\n- `signal.NotifyContext` (SIGINT/SIGTERM), `server.Shutdown(ctx)` with timeout\n\n**Stage 5 — Security Hardening (the track below)**\n\n**Stage 6 — Tests**\n- `httptest.NewServer` / `httptest.NewRecorder` table-driven tests per handler and status code\n\n## 2. Recommended Security Track: \"Defensive Request Processing\"\n\nThis is the best fit for a basic request/response server because every item maps directly to code the AI will generate, and each is a clear place to apply in-class knowledge (HTTP semantics, TCP connections, TLS, resource exhaustion).\n\nTrack components (implement in this order):\n1. **Strict method routing** — reject wrong methods with `405` + `Allow` header (HTTP spec compliance)\n2. **Input validation & sanitization** — validate params/body; reject malformed input with `400`; prevent path traversal (`../`) in any file/ID input\n3. **Secure response headers** — `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `X-Frame-Options`, `Content-Security-Policy`, `Referrer-Policy`, `Strict-Transport-Security` (when TLS)\n4. **Centralized error handling** — generic messages to clients, detailed logs server-side (no info leakage / stack traces)\n5. **Request size limits** — `http.MaxBytesReader` to prevent memory exhaustion\n6. **Rate limiting** — per-IP token bucket (ties to DoS/availability concepts)\n7. **TLS** — `ListenAndServeTLS` with modern cipher suites / `tls.Config` (MinVersion TLS 1.2+)\n8. **Timeouts & connection limits** — `ReadHeaderTimeout`, `MaxHeaderBytes`, `ConnState` hooks (slowloris defense)\n\nWhy this track over others (e.g., auth): auth requires a user store and is a bigger jump; defensive request processing keeps the server \"basic\" while giving many concrete, gradeable improvements that demonstrate understanding of HTTP and transport-layer security.\n\n## 3. Prompting Tips for the Initial AI-Generated Code\n\nUse one **scaffold prompt**, then iterate. Key principles:\n\n- **Pin the stdlib**: \"Use only the Go standard library (`net/http`); no third-party frameworks.\"\n- **State the Go version**: \"Target Go 1.22+ and use the enhanced `ServeMux` patterns.\"\n- **Enumerate endpoints + codes explicitly** with a table:\n  ```\n  GET /health -> 200 {\"status\":\"ok\"}\n  GET /items/{id} -> 200 or 404\n  POST /items -> 201 or 400\n  (any other method on a route -> 405 with Allow header)\n  ```\n- **Ask for structure, not one file**: \"Split into `main.go`, `handlers/`, `middleware/`. Use `http.Handler` interfaces.\"\n- **Request educational comments**: \"Add comments explaining HTTP/networking concepts (e.g., why ReadTimeout matters).\"\n- **Specify non-functional requirements**: graceful shutdown, timeouts, request IDs, structured logging.\n- **Give an example request/response** to anchor behavior.\n- **Constrain scope**: \"No database; use an in-memory map guarded by sync.RWMutex.\" (keeps it basic and testable)\n- **Ask for tests in the same prompt** or a follow-up: \"Include `_test.go` files using `httptest`.\"\n- **Iterate, don't one-shot**: First prompt = scaffold + 2 routes. Then \"add middleware X\", \"add input validation\", etc. Smaller prompts yield cleaner, reviewable diffs.\n- **Avoid over-specifying security in v1**: Let v1 be intentionally naive so the \"improvements\" (Stage 5) are meaningful and demonstrable — your class knowledge is what closes the gap.\n\n### Sample scaffold prompt\n> \"Generate a basic HTTP server in Go 1.22 using only the standard library. Module name `httpserver-lab`. Endpoints: GET /health returns 200 JSON; GET /items/{id} returns 200 or 404; POST /items accepts JSON {name} and returns 201 or 400. Wrong methods return 405 with an Allow header. Unknown routes return 404. Include Read/Write/Idle timeouts, graceful shutdown on SIGINT/SIGTERM, request logging middleware, panic recovery middleware, and in-memory storage with sync.RWMutex. Split into main.go, handlers/, middleware/. Add comments explaining networking/HTTP concepts. Include httptest-based tests for each status code. Keep it intentionally simple so I can harden it later.\"\n\nThen follow-ups: \"Add input validation + path-traversal protection\", \"Add secure headers + MaxBytesReader\", \"Add per-IP rate limiting\", \"Add TLS with modern config\".\n\nWant me to also generate the actual starter code, or refine the prompt into a version tailored to your grading rubric?"
